<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malta is an island country in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, made up of Malta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gozo">Gozo</a> and smaller islands. Its harbours drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, who took it in 870, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a>; from 1530 it was the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>, who withstood the Ottoman Great Siege of 1565 and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valletta">Valletta</a>. A British colony from 1800, it became independent in 1964.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malta.jpg" alt="Malta" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See File history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Malta.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Malta is an archipelago in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, about 80 kilometres from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> across the Malta Channel. Only Malta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gozo">Gozo</a> and Comino have permanent residents, and Comino is practically uninhabited. The islands lie on a shallow shelf that was once part of a land bridge between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, and the archipelago sits on the African tectonic plate. The terrain is coarse and hilly, with many harbours along an indented coastline. The highest point, Ta' Dmejrek near Dingli, is 253 metres high, and Malta has no rivers or lakes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest reliable evidence of habitation dates to 6500 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesolithic">Mesolithic</a> hunter-gatherers, probably from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, arrived. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> farmers from Sicily are thought to have followed by around 5400 BCE. Around 3500 BCE the islanders built the Ġgantija temples on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gozo">Gozo</a>, among the oldest free-standing structures in the world, and other early temples include those at Ħaġar Qim and Mnajdra. After 2500 BCE Malta was depopulated until a new people arrived who cremated their dead and built small dolmens as tombs; they probably differed from the temple builders.</p><p>Phoenician traders colonised the islands around 1000 BCE, with their seat of government at Mdina and their main port at Cospicua on the Grand Harbour, which they called Maleth. Carthage controlled the islands after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>. It is unsure whether Roman rule began after the first or second Punic War, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> held Malta after the second, when it was surrendered by Hamilcar, a son of Gisco, to the consul Titus Sempronius Longus. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> and Aragonese ruled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights Hospitaller</a>, French and British in the modern era.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Malta became a British crown colony in the early 19th century and served as headquarters of the British Mediterranean Fleet. It was besieged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Independence came in 1964, and the current parliamentary republic was established in 1974. Malta joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 2004 and the eurozone on 1 January 2008.</p><p>The two official languages are Maltese, a Semitic language with close ties to Arabic and Sicilian, and English. Catholicism is the state religion, though the constitution guarantees freedom of religion. The 2025 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 588,254 people.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 835 Aghlabid forces campaigned in Calabria and Apulia, taking Amantea, Taranto and Bari, but the destruction of a Muslim fleet off Ostia in 849 halted the advance, and <b>Malta</b> was conquered in 870.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…of Lauria won a series of victories against the Angevins after Peter III took Sicily in 1282: off <b>Malta</b> in 1283, in the Bay of Naples in 1284, where Charles of Salerno was captured, and at Les Formigues in 1285, which wrecked the French invasion known as the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ibn Abbad of Syracuse held out until he died in 1086; Hammud of Castrogiovanni surrendered and converted in 1087, and in 1091 Butera, Noto and <b>Malta</b> fell.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>European settlers came from France, Spain, Italy and <b>Malta</b>; a law of 1889 made the children of foreign Europeans born in Algeria French, and by 1954 there were about a million Europeans, later called pieds-noirs, beside some eight to nine million Muslim Algerians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1530 the Knights Hospitaller held <b>Malta</b> as a fief of the kingdom, paying the king of Sicily an annual tribute of a single falcon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Gaiseric conquered Sicily, Sardinia, Corsica, <b>Malta</b> and the Balearic Islands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
